From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Khanh Truong <khanhtd36@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 12:51:16 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] vendor(libghostty-vt): add prompt-aware terminal
 clear_screen C API

Export ghostty_terminal_clear_screen(): erases the primary screen's
scrollback history always, and its active content according to
whether the cursor is at an idle shell prompt (OSC 133
semantic-prompt state, via the existing upstream
Terminal.cursorIsAtPrompt() -- already used internally by real
Ghostty's own clear_screen keybind action). If at a prompt, the
entire active screen is cleared. Otherwise -- mid-command output, or
no shell integration -- only rows strictly above the cursor's row are
erased, so the screen is never left blank with a stranded cursor when
there is no shell integration to redraw a prompt afterward. It always
targets the primary screen directly (not whichever screen is active),
so it is safe to call while the alternate screen (vim, tmux) is
active. Returns whether the cursor was at an idle prompt, so callers
can decide whether to nudge the shell to redraw its prompt, using the
same prompt determination this function itself used.

The not-at-prompt branch uses Screen.eraseActive, the same primitive
real Ghostty's own Termio.clearScreen uses for this fallback. That
physically removes the rows above the cursor and shifts the survivors
up, so the cursor's row -- typically the shell's prompt -- ends up at
the top of the screen. That shift is the entire mechanism by which
Cmd+K appears to "move the prompt to the top left" with no shell
integration and nothing written to the pty, so it must not be
replaced by an in-place clear such as Screen.clearRows: that erases
the same cells but leaves the prompt stranded at its old row.

eraseActive marks only the shifted rows dirty, not the rows regrown
at the bottom to refill the active area. A differential renderer such
as herdr's therefore never repaints those rows and leaves the erased
content visible below the prompt. This patch marks the whole active
area dirty after the erase so the next render reflects the shift.

Cursor position is never touched explicitly by this function, in
either branch, matching real Ghostty's own clear_screen action
exactly: the at-prompt branch relies entirely on the shell's own
response to a form-feed byte (zle/readline's clear-screen widget) to
redraw and reposition its prompt, and the not-at-prompt branch needs
no repositioning because eraseActive's row shift carries the cursor
to the top on its own. Repositioning the cursor locally first --
before the shell's own response arrives -- would silently move it
without going through the pty, so the shell (which computes its own
repositioning relative to what it still thinks the cursor row is)
ends up fighting that mismatch instead of landing where it should.

Vendored base: c5a21edfcbc2d5b46540ad91b7980aca31f5f1f3
